Each {term:model:model} has its own {term:chat-template:template}. <strong>Using wrong template = broken {term:model:model}!</strong><br><br><strong>LLaMA-2 Format:</strong><br><code class="code-block">&lt;s&gt;[INST] &lt;&lt;SYS&gt;&gt;
You are a math tutor.
&lt;&lt;/SYS&gt;&gt;

What is 2+2? [/INST] The answer is 4. &lt;/s&gt;
&lt;s&gt;[INST] Now multiply by 10 [/INST] 4 × 10 = 40 &lt;/s&gt;</code><br><br><strong>ChatML Format (Mistral, many OSS):</strong><br><code class="code-block">&lt;|im_start|&gt;system
You are a math tutor.&lt;|im_end|&gt;
&lt;|im_start|&gt;user
What is 2+2?&lt;|im_end|&gt;
&lt;|im_start|&gt;assistant
The answer is 4.&lt;|im_end|&gt;</code>

Sometimes you need MORE than what the pretrained {term:model:model} offers:<br><br><code class="code-block"># Add new layers on top of pretrained model
class CustomModel(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, base_model):
        self.base = base_model          # 7B params (pretrained)
        self.custom_head = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Linear(4096, 1024),       # New layer 1
            nn.ReLU(),
            nn.Linear(1024, 256),        # New layer 2
            nn.Linear(256, num_classes)  # Output
        )

    def forward(self, x):
        hidden = self.base(x).last_hidden_state[:, -1, :]
        return self.custom_head(hidden)</code><br><br><strong>Now train EVERYTHING:</strong> base model + new layers together.
